# Development

Run commands from the workspace root:

```bash
uv sync --all-packages
uv run inv -l
uv run inv check
uv run inv format
uv run inv test.all
uv build --all-packages
```

`check` is read-only. `format` applies formatting and import sorting only.
RTK is an external development executable; use `--no-rtk` for direct tool output.
Tests and ty run separately for each member. Root tasks are type-checked too.
Root development dependencies hold tooling; runtime dependencies belong to members.

At substantive task closeout, review [open follow-ups](next-steps.md), add findings with
evidence and completion criteria, remove completed items, update affected docs, and add
user-visible changes to [the changelog](../../CHANGELOG.md). Keep speculative ideas out
unless a concrete benefit and next action are known.

## Commits

Use [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/en/v1.0.0/):
`type(scope): concise summary`. The scope is optional; choose an affected member or
capability when useful. Use `feat` for features and `fix` for bugs. Other useful types are
`docs`, `refactor`, `perf`, `test`, `build`, `ci`, `style`, and `chore`.

```text
feat(cli): add command search
fix(config): reject unknown keys
docs(workspace): explain member ownership
```

Use a short imperative summary and keep commits cohesive. Mark breaking changes with `!`
before the colon or a `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer, and explain the migration. Add a body for
useful rationale, separated by a blank line. Review the staged diff so the message reflects
the actual changes; mention only checks that ran. Apply the same convention to the final
squash commit in squash workflows.
